WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.630
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.370
YUFENG GUO: Machine
learning is awesome,

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.932
except when it forces
you to do advanced math.

00:00:04.932 --> 00:00:06.390
The tools for
machine learning have

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:09.090
gotten dramatically better,
and training your own model

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.700
has never been easier.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.020
We'll utilize our
understanding of our dataset,

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.930
rather than an understanding
of the raw mathematics,

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.750
to build a model that
gets us insights.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:20.610
Welcome to Cloud AI Adventures.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.080
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.310
On this show, we'll
explore the art, science,

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:27.220
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.680
While machine learning might
be a great conversation

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:33.790
topic at dinner parties, it's
even more useful with code.

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:36.850
In this episode, we're going to
train a simple classifier using

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.140
only a handful lines of code.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:42.340
In fact, here's all the code
that we'll look at today.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.140
To train our
classifier, we'll use

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:47.770
TensorFlow, Google's open
source machine learning library.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.620
TensorFlow has a pretty
large API surface,

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.230
but the part we care about
today is just the high level

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:55.750
APIs called estimators.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:58.150
Estimators package up
the training loop for us

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:01.090
so that we can train a model
by configuring it, rather

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:02.950
than coding it by hand.

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:04.900
This takes away much
of the boilerplate,

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.052
allowing us to think at a
higher level of abstraction.

00:01:08.052 --> 00:01:10.510
And this means we'll get to
play with the interesting parts

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:14.770
of machine learning, and not get
too bogged down in the details.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:17.870
Since we've only covered linear
models so far in the series,

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:20.900
we will stick with that here,
and revisit this example

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.860
in the future to extend
its capabilities.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.590
So with that, let's
dive right in and

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:29.700
go to our Jupiter notebook.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.495
OK, so here we are in
our Jupiter notebook.

00:01:32.495 --> 00:01:33.870
And the first
thing that we'll do

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:37.544
is import TensorFlow
as tf and numpy as mp.

00:01:37.544 --> 00:01:39.710
I like to print out the
version number of TensorFlow

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:42.240
below to confirm which
version I'm using.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.740
This week, we'll
be building a model

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.440
to distinguish between three
different types of very

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:47.730
similar flowers.

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:50.190
I realize that this may be
less interesting than the beer

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.110
and wine from the
previous episode,

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:55.110
but these flowers are a bit
more difficult to distinguish,

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.390
making this a more
interesting challenge.

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:00.060
In particular, we'll be
classifying different species

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.860
of iris flowers.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.650
Now, I'm not sure I could pick
out an iris flower from a field

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:08.030
of roses, but our model is
aiming to distinguish iris

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:12.030
setosa, iris versicolour,
and iris virginica.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:13.830
We have a dataset
of measurements

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:17.700
of the height and width of these
flowers as petals and sepals.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.250
These four columns will
serve as our features.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:24.490
So let's load our dataset
in using TensorFlow's Load

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:26.400
CSV with Header function.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.220
The data or features are
presented as floating point

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.700
numbers, and the label for
each row of data or target

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.420
is recorded as an integer--

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:39.000
0, 1, or 2-- corresponding to
our three species of flowers.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.100
Now I've printed out the
results for our loading,

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.160
and we can see that we are
now able to access the trained

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.310
data and the associated
labels or targets

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:49.530
using named attributes.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:50.910
Next, we'll build the model.

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:54.270
To do this, we'll first
set up the feature columns.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:57.510
Feature columns define the types
of data coming into the model.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.090
We are using a
four-dimensional feature column

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:05.250
to represent our features, and
calling them flower features.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:08.430
Building our model using
estimators is super simple.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:11.850
Using
tf.estimators.LinearClassifier,

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:14.850
we can instantiate the model by
passing in the future columns

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:16.360
we just created.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:18.750
The number of different outputs
that the model predicts--

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:20.100
in this case, 3--

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.240
and a directory to store
the model's training

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.351
progress and the output files.

00:03:24.351 --> 00:03:26.100
This allows TensorFlow
to pick up training

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.900
later on from where it
left off, if needed.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.910
This classifier object will
keep track of state for us.

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:36.560
And we are now almost ready
to move on to the training.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:38.600
There is just one
final piece to connect

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:42.110
our model to the training data,
and that is the input function.

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:44.090
The job of the input
function is to create

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:47.540
the TensorFlow operations that
generate data for the model.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:50.870
So we go from raw data
to the input function,

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:54.110
which passes that data that
is then mapped by the feature

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:56.730
columns to go into the model.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:59.510
Notice that we use the same
name for the features as we

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:01.820
did in defining
the feature column.

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:04.250
This is how the
data is associated.

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:06.530
Now it's time to
run our training.

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:10.550
To train our model, we'll
just run classifier.train

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.310
with the input function
passed in as an argument.

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:16.810
This is how we connect
our dataset to the model.

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:19.459
The train function handles
the training loop and iterates

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:23.840
over the dataset, improving
its performance with each step.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:28.130
And just like that, we've
completed 1,000 training steps.

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:31.950
Our dataset's not huge, so
this completed rather quickly.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:34.280
Now it's time to
evaluate our results.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:38.030
We can do this using the same
classifier object from before,

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:41.420
as it holds the trained
state of the model.

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:43.520
To determine how
good our model is,

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:48.050
we run classifier.evaluate
and pass in our test dataset.

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:50.750
Then we can extract the
accuracy from the metrics

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:51.950
that are returned.

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:54.490
96.6%.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:55.010
OK.

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:58.370
Let's pause here and review
what we've achieved so far.

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:01.130
The estimator's API has
given us a nice workflow

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:04.010
of getting our raw data and
passing it through an input

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:08.210
function, setting up our feature
columns and model structure,

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:10.040
running our training,
and finally,

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:12.090
running our evaluation.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:13.550
This easy-to-understand
framework

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:16.850
allows us to think about
our data and its properties,

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:21.980
rather than the underlying math,
which is a great place to be.

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:23.840
Today, we looked at
a very simple version

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:27.500
of TensorFlow's high level
API using a canned estimator.

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:29.150
In future episodes,
we'll look at how

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:33.140
to augment this model with more
details, use more complex data,

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:34.880
and even more advanced features.

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:40.490
Thanks for watching that
episode of Cloud AI Adventures.

